@* The opt-in behind the app's only unauthenticated write: letting a tablet bolted to THIS wall
   create a boulder with nobody signed in. Off by default and off for every existing wall, so a gym
   that never turns it on has no anonymous write surface at all.

   Admin-only, and never rendered on a kiosk session — the wall is administered from an admin's own
   device. That hiding is cosmetic; WallService.SetAnonymousKioskSettingAsync refuses a kiosk caller
   and re-checks wall admin against the database. *@
@using Blocwerk.Core.Helpers
@inject IWallService WallService

<div class="settings-panel wall-kiosk-anon-panel">
    <p class="apikey-blurb">
        Normally a tablet needs somebody to tap their name before anything can be set. Turn this on
        and the wall's tablet can also publish a boulder with <em>nobody</em> picked — useful when a
        setting crew is working and nobody wants to log in between problems.
    </p>

    <p class="apikey-blurb">
        Boulders set that way are credited to <strong>@PlaceholderIdentity.DisplayName</strong>
        unless a setter is picked from the list, and only people who consented to this wall's kiosk
        can be picked. Anyone with physical access to the tablet can do this, so leave it off unless
        the tablet is somewhere you're happy with that.
    </p>

    @if (_message is not null)
    {
        <div class="wall-kiosk-pair-ok" role="status">@_message</div>
    }

    <label class="checkbox-label">
        <input type="checkbox" checked="@_allowed" disabled="@_busy"
               @onchange="e => ToggleAsync(e.Value is true)" />
        Let this wall's tablet set boulders without signing in
    </label>
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>The wall being administered. The ONLY wall this card can ever change.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public Guid WallId { get; set; }

    private bool _allowed;
    private bool _busy;
    private string? _message;

    protected override async Task OnInitializedAsync()
    {
        // Read the stored value so the box opens on the truth. A failure here (a wall the caller
        // cannot read) leaves it unticked, which is the safe way to be wrong.
        try
        {
            var wall = await WallService.GetWallAsync(WallId);
            _allowed = wall?.AllowAnonymousKioskSetting ?? false;
        }
        catch (Exception)
        {
            _allowed = false;
        }
    }

    private async Task ToggleAsync(bool allowed)
    {
        if (_busy)
        {
            return;
        }

        _busy = true;
        _message = null;

        try
        {
            await WallService.SetAnonymousKioskSettingAsync(WallId, allowed);
            _allowed = allowed;
            _message = allowed
                ? "On. The tablet can now set boulders with nobody signed in."
                : "Off. The tablet needs somebody picked before it can set anything.";
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            // Leave the box showing the state that actually holds, not the change that failed.
            _allowed = !allowed;
            _message = ex.Message;
        }
        finally
        {
            _busy = false;
        }
    }
}
